Bufferbloat - PS2
=================

Instructions to reproduce the results
-------------------------------------

Prerequisites:
  - Mininet (tested with mn 2.3+ on Linux 5.x, Python 3.8)
  - Open vSwitch (used as the switch; no OpenFlow controller needed)
  - iperf (v2), curl, ss, tc (iproute2)
  - Python 3 with matplotlib:    pip3 install matplotlib

Run the full experiment (both queue sizes, all three plots each):

    sudo ./run.sh

This produces two output directories, bb-q100 and bb-q20, each
containing cwnd.txt, q.txt, ping.txt, fetch_stats.txt and the three
PNG plots (cwnd-iperf.png, q.png, rtt.png).  Each run lasts ~200 s.

Compatibility notes: the skeleton was ported from Python 2 to Python 3
(http.server / socketserver, list comprehensions in place of map, etc.).
The tcp_probe kernel module is gone from modern kernels, so cwnd is
sampled by polling `ss -tin` for the iperf flow inside h1's namespace
every 0.1 s (see start_cwnd_monitor in bufferbloat.py); plot_tcpprobe.py
reads that CSV.  The switch is an OVSBridge (standalone learning
bridge) so no OpenFlow controller is required.


Answers
------------------------

1. Why do you see a difference in webpage fetch times with short and
   large router buffers?

   The long-lived iperf flow uses TCP Reno, which only backs off when a
   packet is dropped, and the tail-drop queue only drops when it is
   full.  So Reno grows cwnd until the bottleneck buffer is full and
   keeps it full on average.  A curl GET sent into that full buffer
   sits behind every queued byte and has to wait for them to drain at
   1.5 Mb/s before its own bytes leave the router.  A larger buffer
   means more bytes queued in front of the curl request, which means a
   larger added queueing delay on every SYN, request, and response
   packet of the fetch.  With Q=100 packets (~150 KB) the worst-case
   queueing delay is ~800 ms per traversal; with Q=20 (~30 KB) it is
   ~160 ms.  Fetch time scales accordingly.

2. NIC transmit queue length and drain time.

   `ip link show` on this VM reports `qlen 1000` for the primary NIC.  
   With full 1500-byte ethernet frames the queue can hold

       1000 * 1500 B = 1,500,000 B = 12,000,000 bits.

   Draining at 100 Mb/s = 10^8 bit/s, the maximum time a packet can
   wait in this queue before leaving the NIC is

       12 * 10^6 / 10^8 = 0.12 s = 120 ms.

3. How does the RTT reported by ping vary with the queue size?

   When the bottleneck buffer is saturated by the bulk flow, a ping
   reply has to wait behind every packet already queued.  Let

       RTT_prop = minimum (propagation + transmission) RTT (~20 ms)
       Q = bottleneck queue size, in packets
       MTU = packet size in bits (~1500 B = 12000 bits)
       B = bottleneck bandwidth (1.5 Mb/s)

   Then the maximum RTT seen by ping is

       RTT_max  =  RTT_prop  +  Q * MTU / B

4. Two ways to mitigate bufferbloat.

   (a) Size buffers to the bandwidth-delay product instead of "as big
       as possible".  A buffer only needs ~1 BDP to keep a single TCP
       flow at full throughput; anything larger just adds latency.

   (b) Use an active queue management in place of plain tail-drop.  These drop
       or ECN-mark packets based on how long they have been sitting in
       the queue, so loss-based TCPs back off before the queue fills,
       holding queueing delay to a few milliseconds regardless of the
       physical buffer size.